ID: B3-Ch05a-Intro
TEXT:

"Ah, back at last," the king of the underground says, without looking up from the paper in his hands.

He stands near the entrance of the rebel base, skimming a runner's report with half an eye. Before I can answer, he drops a question that stops me cold.

"I assume negotiations with the Overseer are concluded?"

"I don't know what you mean," I say, keeping my voice level. "I thought our meeting with the Overseer was set for tomorrow."

The king sighs, lowers the parchment, and fixes me with a tired stare.

"Listen to me," he says, rubbing at the bridge of his nose. "Things are grim enough without us wasting breath on theater. I will give it to you plain. I expected you to go behind my back from the moment we first spoke. That is why I had Breynan stick to you. An insurance policy, to make sure your private dealings did not end in disaster for this city."

"Breynan," Kate says, her brow furrowing. "Did you—"

"I didn't say a word, I swear it!" Breynan says quickly. "I haven't spoken to the king since we left!"

"You misunderstand, Kate," the king says. "I gave Breynan no orders. He went of his own free will, not as my spy. What tipped my hand was you."

"Me?" Kate says.

"You," the king replies. "From our first exchange, your eyes made your opinion of me entirely clear. That would hardly matter if the healer led your company. But he made it plain that you decide things by consensus, and my instincts told me you would never willingly join hands with my men. So at the end of that meeting, I forbade Breynan from speaking with the Overseer. I knew precisely how he would take the slight—and that if he saw you heading for the surface, he would follow."

The memory clicks into place: the king glancing toward Kate at the close of our first council, a heartbeat before telling Breynan he was barred from the summit. The man read our move before we had even debated making it.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Intro2
TEXT:

"What kind of absurd gamble is that?" Melindra says. "You sidelined the one man who actually understands the Overseer on nothing more than a hunch? What if you guessed wrong? What if Daren talked us into waiting for morning and marching up with your soldiers?"

The king gives a dry shrug.

"Then I would have told Breynan I changed my mind and sent him along after all," he says. "Besides, it was hardly a blind guess. Every step you took afterward proved me right. You never set down your packs, even in the quarters we gave you, and the ice mage spent our second council broadcasting her contempt. By then I knew you would seek out the machine on your own. That was why I proposed an alliance of convenience rather than trust—one where either side is free to cut ties the second our interests diverge. You recall the terms, I trust?"

"So you are saying it is your turn to strike from behind?" Illuna asks.

"Good heavens, no," the king says. "I have no quarrel with you. Breynan may question my judgment, but his loyalty to Ollendor is beyond dispute. If you had done anything to endanger our people, he would not be standing beside you in silence. That is enough for me. Your healer has spent every waking hour in the quarantine ward keeping my men alive. Treating you as enemies would be an insult to his work."

"Does this mean you don't care what we discussed with the Overseer?" Kate asks.

"Of course I care," the king says. "I intend to ask. You are simply under no obligation to answer. So—will you share the outcome of your summit, or keep it to yourselves?"

choice(""Of course not."", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 0)
choice(""I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 1)
choice(""Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."", B3-Ch05a-Price, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Price, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Price
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."

"A price?" The king pauses, weighing the offer. "To be blunt, I have no intention of emptying my coffers for stale intelligence. Either you struck a clandestine pact with the Overseer—in which case you will lie to my face—or you discussed ways to corner the king, all of which collapsed the moment he locked down the city. Still, I will offer ten gold coins upfront if you confirm or deny my assessment. What do you say?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2) {
"Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."

Kate and Breynan both flinch toward me, then catch themselves, schooling their expressions before they give anything away. The king keeps his eyes on me, oblivious to the exchange.

"A price?" The king pauses, weighing the offer. "To be blunt, I have no intention of emptying my coffers for stale intelligence. Either you struck a clandestine pact with the Overseer—in which case you will lie to my face—or you discussed ways to corner the king, all of which collapsed the moment he locked down the city. Still, I will offer ten gold coins upfront if you confirm or deny my assessment. What do you say?"

}
choice("Confirm that the information from the Overseer has become irrelevant.", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 3)
choice("Tell him that ten gold coins is too small a sum to be worth any answer.", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 4)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Contacting
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch5_opportunist,1) if (v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 3)
achievement("Opportunist",v_ac_b3_ch5_opportunist)
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."

"I am listening," the king says.

"Not much to tell," I say. "The Overseer pointed out that the king has been slipping out of the palace on regular circuits. We were supposed to shadow his scouts, track his movements, and lay an ambush along his route. But that was before he sealed the gates. Whatever schedule he was keeping is useless now."

"As I suspected," the king murmurs. "Even so, I appreciate the candor. Matters are difficult enough without having to second-guess our guests. Get some sleep, all of you. Tomorrow will demand everything we have. We speak again at dawn."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1) {
"I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."

Kate and Breynan snap their heads toward me for a fraction of a second, stiffening before they remember to hold still. The king doesn't notice, his attention fixed squarely on me.

"I am listening," the king says.

"Not much to tell," I say. "The Overseer pointed out that the king has been slipping out of the palace on regular circuits. We were supposed to shadow his scouts, track his movements, and lay an ambush along his route. But that was before he sealed the gates. Whatever schedule he was keeping is useless now."

"As I suspected," the king murmurs. "Even so, I appreciate the candor. Matters are difficult enough without having to second-guess our guests. Get some sleep, all of you. Tomorrow will demand everything we have. We speak again at dawn."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 0) {
"Of course not."

The king shrugs.

"Suit yourself," he says. "It is not as though I cannot piece together the broad strokes on my own. Go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 3) {
"We have a deal," I say. "You guessed right. The Overseer's intelligence is obsolete. I could give you the particulars, but since you only paid for a confirmation, I assume you don't need them."

"Correct," the king says. "I have no use for expired plans. But I appreciate the honesty. Here is your coin."

He counts ten gold pieces into my hand, and I drop them into my belt pouch.

"Now that business is settled," the king says, "go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 4) {
"Ten gold coins isn't worth the breath it takes to answer," I say. "Keep your money."

The king shrugs.

"As you like," he says. "I see little point in turning down free coin, but I will not waste time haggling. Go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 0 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 4 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 0 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 4) {
"Is there anything you need of me, Sire?" Breynan asks.

"You can start by taking half these reports," the king says, gesturing toward an aide holding a thick bundle of parchment. "Take from the bottom. Those should be clear enough even without an intimate knowledge of the city grid. We must relocate as many civilians as possible before sunrise, so prepare for a long night. We will be here until dawn."

"Understood," Breynan says, drawing a sheet from the pile.

We leave them to their work and turn down the tunnel toward our quarters. The waiting hall is crowded with displaced families, rebel runners steering groups toward whatever empty corner remains. Space is clearly running out; several escorts are already guiding refugees toward the mess hall instead of the barracks.

"For a second back there, I thought you were about to mention our other arrangement with the Overseer," Kate whispers once we are out of earshot.

"Give me some credit," I mutter back. "I'm not that reckless."

}
