}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8) {
"Hard to tell," I say. "Depends on whether it keeps its word. At the very least, it wants the king dead just as badly as we do."

"To the abyss with the machine!" Hadrik says. "It's iron and wire. The rebels have the steel. Can we trust them to mount a raid?"

"The rebels won't touch the towers," Kate says, leaning against the doorway. "Their king made that clear. He won't spend a single soldier on a rescue, even knowing innocent people are strapped to the drain. The only thing an alliance gets us is steel against the king on the surface. Nothing more."

"Here is a novel thought," Arraka sneers. "Stop begging help from every pathetic mortal in a ten-mile radius and solve your own messes. Do you think these gutter rebels will hold your hand when you face the God of Time? Will they stand beside you when my five-thousand-year-old self reaches down your throats and pulls your spines out through your teeth? When will you mongrels understand that this is not a game? Take your drills seriously, or die like the amateurs you are."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0) {
"Not a chance," I say. "If anything, it's going to be one of our worst headaches. We cannot afford to drop our guard around it."

"To the abyss with the machine!" Hadrik says. "It's iron and wire. The rebels have the steel. Can we trust them to mount a raid?"

