choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Initiative, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Initiative)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Initiative
TEXT:

"We HAVE been training!" Hadrik roars, his beard bristling. "Every single day since the altar! What more do you want from us, you dried-up old ghoul?"

"I want initiative!" Arraka's voice rattles the metal casing. "I want you to break past your miserable limits instead of pacing inside them like tethered goats! You are aiming to slaughter a GOD! How do you expect to scratch divinity when two royal bodyguards leave you groveling in the dirt?"

Kate leans her back against the rough rock, arms folded tight across her chest, staring down at the gold pendant. "You treat everything as a joke, Arraka. Everything except this. Why does killing the God of Time matter so much to you?"

"Because it isn't a joke, you simpleton!" Arraka snaps, the laughter vanishing from her tone. "Do you have any concept of how many millennia I have waited to settle the score with those posturing parasites on the magical plane? Ripping them apart myself would be too gentle. But having them dismantled by a wandering troupe of mortals? That is the purest, foulest humiliation possible for a creature of pure ether. Barry-boy handed me that prize, and I have no intention of letting you squander it. We kill Selkram, or I tear you to pieces myself."

"While it is refreshing to hear you have a plan," Daren says dryly, "do you intend to offer useful instruction, or just scream insults until morning?"

"You want instruction?" Arraka snarls. "Fine. Dwarf! What was that pathetic display in the cavern today? Gontrok whispered two words and your spine turned to tallow! Is that all your warrior pride amounts to? Two words, and you regress into a drunken brawler? If I say them right now, will you crawl under the bed and weep? Dwarf village. Dwarf village. Dwarf village. Going to shed a tear, little giant?"

"Arraka," Daren growls, stepping between the amulet and the dwarf, his hand dropping to his hilt. "One more word and I swear—"

"No," Hadrik mutters, staring at the floor stones. "She's right. I let him get inside my head. If Barry hadn't stalled Gontrok while I was sprawled in the dirt, you'd all be corpses, and the blood would be on my hands. It won't happen twice."

"It had better not," Arraka says. "If we reach Selkram's throat and you freeze because a stray memory rattles your skull, I will gouge your eyes out and pull your head off your shoulders. And while we're on the subject, use the new forms you've been drilling at night! You spent three days practicing shifts and didn't use a single one when the hammer fell. Were you waiting for an engraved invitation?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Icicles, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Icicles)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Icicles
TEXT:

"I didn't realize you were working on new techniques, Hadrik," Daren says. "You could have asked me to spar."

"Your story about that scar got me thinking," Hadrik says, rubbing at the back of his neck. "Made me realize I've been coasting on brute strength and ignoring my blind spots. I started testing a few shifts to cover the gaps. They weren't polished enough to trust in a live melee today—which is how Gontrok caught me flat-footed—but I'll drill them until they're second nature tonight. No more excuses."

"Splendid," Arraka says. "Now quiet down. I'm not finished handing out corrections. Kate! What is your aversion to freezing magic? Are you allergic to your own element? I have barely seen you cast a true freeze since we met, even though flash-freezing is the deadliest tool an elementalist possesses against other mages. It is always icicles with you. Drop the monotony and diversify."

"Icicles are efficient," Kate says, her voice flat. "They carry range, mass, and velocity. Conjuring them high above the line makes them hard to read until it's too late. It works. I see no reason to discard what works."

"Do not play coy with me," Arraka snaps. "I asked why you avoid the frost itself. Even when an ice beam would have ended a duel in a heartbeat, you default to physical spikes. That isn't efficiency. What are you hiding?"

Kate glares at the amulet, her jaw working before she answers.

"Control," she says quietly. "I have full command over conjured ice. A spike, a wall, a construct—I shape them, guide them, and disperse them when the fight is done. A freezing beam is different. Once the temperature drops and the flesh turns to frost, I cannot unfreeze them. I have no counter-spell. Icicles give me precision; freezing spells bloom across an area. If a companion steps into the path by mistake, the damage is permanent. I only use the frost when I am certain the lane is clear."

"Excuses," Arraka scoffs. "You don't know how to thaw your own work? Then learn. It isn't complicated."

"I've tried," Kate says through clenched teeth. "Dozens of times. It doesn't answer."

"Then try harder!" Arraka snaps. "Or ask an elementalist who actually understands the art! You have an ethereal sleeping on the floor ten feet away. Do you have any idea how fast ethereals grasp magical flow? That child absorbs in an afternoon what takes human academics a decade to decipher. Ask her for a foundation."

choice(""Do you think that Flower's ethereal knowledge extends also to ice magic?"", B3-Ch05a-Knowledge, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Knowledge)
choice(""Is Flower really that talented? How did she not become insanely strong by now?"", B3-Ch05a-Talented, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Talented)
choice(""Why don't you tell Kate how to unfreeze people if you're so smart?"", B3-Ch05a-Unfreeze, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Unfreeze)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Knowledge
TEXT:

"Do you think that Flower's ethereal knowledge extends also to ice magic?"

"Doubtful, but irrelevant," Arraka says. "Elemental shaping follows the same underlying currents whether you're throwing frost, fire, or lightning. Even without an ice affinity, Flower understands how ethereal energy grips the physical plane. She can show Kate where the disconnect sits."

"Will mastering the frost help against the God of Time?" I ask.

"Not directly," Arraka says. "But it breaks Kate out of her predictable routines. She cannot rely on long-range artillery forever. Flower can supply fresh concepts—the brat has an annoying knack for improvisation when she isn't terrified. Now, the captain of the royal guard. That old butcher is a more immediate problem. Ideally I would drill all of you in countering his aura compression, but we don't have the hours. We may run into him again tomorrow. So the solution is simple: when he steps into the street, leave him to Daren."

"Me?" Daren blinks.

"Yes, you!" Arraka snaps. "You finally grasped how to untangle aura flows, didn't you? When he tries to drown you in his aura and pin you to the pavement, you unravel the weave. You slip right through the bind. It will be considerably harder against a seasoned killer than against dying patients, but that enchanted blade of yours cuts magical conduits. Cut the knots with the sword. You understand the principle, don't you?"

Daren looks down at his gauntleted hand, flexing his fingers. "Yes... I believe I do."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Good," Arraka says. "That is enough lecturing for one night. Go to sleep."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Good," Arraka says. "That is enough lecturing for one night. Hmm... though while we are on the subject—Rose, are you still on the line?"

Silence answers from the transceiver. Arraka raises her volume until the brass casing vibrates.

"ROSE! Wake up!"

A shuffle of footsteps echoes over the speaker, followed by Rose's breathless voice.

"I'm here!" she says. "Suzie woke up with a nightmare, so I took her some sweet biscuits from the kitchen. Did I miss anything?"

"Nothing vital," Arraka says. "Did Barry explain what you must do before the next ritual?"

"Yes," Rose says hesitantly. "I have to prevent the God of Time from siphoning my power again. I've been practicing, but..."

"You don't know if the ward holds?"

"I think I understand the shape of it," Rose murmurs. "It's just difficult to know if it works without an enemy to test it against."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Lockdown, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Lockdown) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Reverse, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Reverse) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Talented
TEXT:

"Is Flower really that talented? How did she not become insanely strong by now?"

"She IS strong by mortal standards," Arraka says. "You cannot compare her to Melindra or that earth sage—those two were born with an absurd reservoir of raw power. Natural instinct only carries you so far. Reaching the upper tiers requires grinding through theory and grimoires, which Flower is utterly incapable of doing. But that doesn't matter tonight. What matters is that she understands elemental resonance from the soul side, so she can give Kate the foundation she lacks."

"Will mastering the frost help against the God of Time?" I ask.

"Not directly," Arraka says. "But it breaks Kate out of her predictable routines. She cannot rely on long-range artillery forever. Flower can supply fresh concepts—the brat has an annoying knack for improvisation when she isn't terrified. Now, the captain of the royal guard. That old butcher is a more immediate problem. Ideally I would drill all of you in countering his aura compression, but we don't have the hours. We may run into him again tomorrow. So the solution is simple: when he steps into the street, leave him to Daren."

