ID: B3-Ch05a-Reverse
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0) {
"Do not fret over it," Arraka says. "Grasping the intent is enough for now. Once the foundation settles, I will give you a proper method to test your wards. But the real trial arrives at the next ritual. By then you must be sharp enough to sever Selkram's first siphon on your reversal magic without alerting him. He will stumble, but he will write it off as a tremor in the link. Once you know your defense holds, drop the ward and let him draw the remainder. We cannot fight him yet; we preserve the trap until the third ceremony. By then, with luck, this rabble will be marginally less useless."

"Is... that all you need of me?" Rose asks.

"No," Arraka says. "I want clarity on your progress. I hear you caught a glimpse of the future and managed to hold time still long enough for these idiots to bypass a hall of traps. Can you decelerate time yet? What about rewinding it?"

"Oh, heavens no," Rose says. "I cannot slow time, let alone reverse it. I only attempted the freeze because the mechanics seemed the most straightforward in my memory. Slowing time is far beyond me. As for rewinding... I couldn't command that consciously even while anchored to the Magium. I still don't know how it triggered when you breached the amulet. And the premonition arrived without warning. I did nothing to summon it."

"Premonitions are simply your senses adapting to temporal currents," Arraka says. "You will never trigger them on command. Normally they warn of threats to your own hide, not strangers. That your first instinct was shielding Illuna suggests you spend entirely too much energy fretting over others. Still—keep drilling. If you maintain this pace, you may prove more lethal than every warrior in this room. A god flails like a child when the seconds stop moving around him. Delicious irony for a deity of time."

"I don't know if I can hold him," Rose murmurs. "Holding thirty seconds today nearly made me faint."

"On the earthen plane, yes," Arraka says. "During the ritual you will be tethered to the Magium, with vast reservoirs to draw upon. Keep practicing. And do not neglect the deflection drills."

"I will," Rose says. "Thank you, Arraka."

"Good. Class is over. Go to sleep."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 0) {
"Do not fret over it," Arraka says. "Grasping the intent is enough for now. Once the foundation settles, I will give you a proper method to test your wards. But the real trial arrives at the next ritual. By then you must be sharp enough to sever Selkram's first siphon on your reversal magic without alerting him. He will stumble, but he will write it off as a tremor in the link. Once you know your defense holds, drop the ward and let him draw the remainder. We cannot fight him yet; we preserve the trap until the third ceremony. By then, with luck, this rabble will be marginally less useless."

"Is... that all you need of me?" Rose asks.

"No," Arraka says. "I want clarity on your progress. I hear you caught a premonition of Illuna's death earlier today. That doesn't happen unless your temporal channels are already stabilizing. Tell me exactly how far you have progressed."

"Well..." Rose begins softly. "I managed to freeze time today after hours of focus, but only briefly. The strain is immense. Whenever I try to anchor the field, my head spins and my vision goes dark. I managed three short bursts, none lasting more than thirty seconds."

"Thirty seconds?" Hadrik's eyes widen. "By the forge, that's magnificent!"

"So you can halt the flow on command," Arraka purrs, intrigued. "Unexpected. Can you decelerate the seconds, then? What about rewinding them?"

"Oh, heavens no," Rose says. "I cannot slow time, let alone reverse it. I only attempted the freeze because the mechanics seemed the most straightforward in my memory. Slowing time is far beyond me. As for rewinding... I couldn't command that consciously even while anchored to the Magium. I still don't know how it triggered when you breached the amulet. And the premonition arrived without warning. I did nothing to summon it."

"Premonitions are simply your senses adapting to temporal currents," Arraka says. "You will never trigger them on command. Normally they warn of threats to your own hide, not strangers. That your first instinct was shielding Illuna suggests you spend entirely too much energy fretting over others. Still—keep drilling. If you maintain this pace, you may prove more lethal than every warrior in this room. A god flails like a child when the seconds stop moving around him. Delicious irony for a deity of time."

"I don't know if I can hold him," Rose murmurs. "Like I said, thirty seconds pushed me to the brink of fainting."

"On the earthen plane, yes," Arraka says. "During the ritual you will be tethered to the Magium, with vast reservoirs to draw upon. Keep practicing. And do not neglect the deflection drills."

"I will," Rose says. "Thank you, Arraka."

"Good. Class is over. Go to sleep."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Lockdown, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Lockdown)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Lockdown
TEXT:

"Praise the ancestors," Hadrik mutters to Daren under his breath. "Thought she'd lecture until sunrise."

"I have ears, dwarf," Arraka snaps from the pendant in Daren's grip. "I am sitting three inches from your nose."

Hadrik grins into his beard. "I know."

"If you are finished with the wardenship, I will take the chain," Illuna says, stepping forward. "Unless you intend to spend the night in the infirmary."

"No," Daren sighs, handing over the brass pendant. "I need rest. Take her."

Illuna slips the heavy chain over her neck, settling the amulet against her tunic.

"I need to be on the surface early," Daren says, unbuckling his gauntlets. "If we're fortunate, the apothecaries will unbar their shutters for an hour or two before the guard patrols tighten the cordon."

choice(""Do you not have any spells that could heal these people without requiring herbs?"", B3-Ch05a-Herbs, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Herbs)
choice(""Why do you think the lockdown will get worse?"", B3-Ch05a-Worse, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Worse)
choice(""Here's hoping that the shopkeepers won't charge extra for being open during lockdown."", B3-Ch05a-Shopkeepers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Shopkeepers)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Herbs
TEXT:

"Do you not have any spells that could heal these people without requiring herbs?"

"No standard white magic touches this plague," Daren says, setting his armor aside. "I am trying to alter the resonance of an existing cure so it purges the contagion, but shaping the conduit requires specific catalysts. Without the reagents, the magic simply washes over them."

"One question," Melindra says, leaning against the doorframe. "When you say you're heading to the surface at dawn, did you mean alone?"

"Of course," Daren says. "I will not drag you into royal guard patrols for a burden I chose to shoulder. This is my responsibility."

"The hell it is," Melindra snaps. "If today proved anything, it's that scattering across the map gets people killed. The streets are swarming with search parties. If you walk up those stairs, we all go. Right, Kate?"

"She's right," Kate says, her arms still folded. "We cannot afford another divided line. Not under curfew."

"Exactly," Melindra adds. "Besides, Illuna would probably strangle us in our sleep if she had to stage a second rescue in twenty-four hours."

"That is accurate," Illuna says without looking up.

Daren holds up his hands in surrender. "Fine, fine. We go together. Now get some sleep. Morning will come fast enough."

"Illuna and I will take first watch," I say, stepping toward the tunnel entrance as the others start laying out bedrolls.

Daren frowns. "First watch? Barry, we are behind fortified rebel lines."

"I agree with Barry," Illuna says coldly. "I have little faith in this underground king, and our isolation in this corner of the compound is hardly accidental. A sentry at the archway costs nothing."

"Second watch is mine, then," Kate says. "A small return for the trouble I caused today."

Leila traces glowing blue letters in the dim air, smiling faintly: "I will take second watch with Kate."

"Settled," I say. "Four hours each. Kate, Leila, we will wake you when the time is up. Come on, Illuna."

Melindra narrows her eyes, watching us slip through the doorway with an arched brow.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Volume, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Volume) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Guilty, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Guilty) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Worse
TEXT:

"Why do you think the lockdown will get worse?"

"Because the entire curfew exists to smoke us out," Daren says. "What happens when a full day passes and the garrison has turned up nothing?"

"They can't keep the city shuttered indefinitely," I say. "People need food and trade, or the city starves."

"The tyrant on that throne doesn't care if commoners starve," Daren replies grimly. "His palace vaults are stocked for years. In any case, I need to reach the markets the moment dawn breaks, so I am turning in. If there is nothing else—"

"One question," Melindra says, leaning against the doorframe. "When you say you're heading to the surface at dawn, did you mean alone?"

